From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 20:14:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] backlight: exynos9820-dsim-lcd: Add panel power over the
 boot-loader DSI link

Until the DECON and DSI drivers of the Exynos9820 run on hardware, a board
built around it shows the simple-framebuffer its boot loader leaves
behind: DSIM0 in command mode, and DECON0 sending that buffer to the panel
on every tearing effect pulse.  Blanking the framebuffer then paints it
black and nothing more, while the panel keeps scanning.

Add an LCD class driver that sends the DCS display off and display on
commands over that link.  fb blanking notifies LCD class devices, so
console blanking now turns the picture of the panel off and unblanking
turns it back on.  With the default VESA mode console blanking arrives as
LCD_POWER_REDUCED rather than LCD_POWER_OFF, so anything but LCD_POWER_ON
counts as off.

A command goes out the way dsim_write_data() of the vendor driver sends a
short DCS write: one header written to the packet header queue with the
bits above the header kept, then a wait for the queue to drain, with the
same 50 ms the vendor driver allows.  A header only ever goes into an
empty queue, since a queue that does not drain has no reset short of
initialising the link again.  Once it is in the queue it counts as sent,
as it does for the vendor driver: a late drain or an error bit is only
reported.  Nothing else of the link, its D-PHY or DECON is touched.

A command that finds the queue busy is retried from a work item, a few
times 100 ms apart: the LCD core drops what set_power returns, and the
console does not ask a second time once it thinks it is unblanked, so a
lost display on would otherwise leave the panel dark under a running
system.  Both commands are sent on every notification, being idempotent.

Sleep in is left out: the vendor driver cuts the panel supplies after it
and runs the whole panel initialisation on the way back.

The registers this needs are added to exynos9820_regs.h, next to the ones
the DSI driver takes from there.

Measured on a Galaxy S10+ with an out-of-tree module doing the same
writes: both commands leave the queue with no error bit set, the panel
goes dark on 0x28 and shows the same picture again on 0x29, and tearing
effect pulses keep coming at 60 Hz while the picture is off, so DECON is
not left waiting for one.  With this driver built in, every blank and
unblank of the console, from the power key, from a double tap and from
the 30 s idle timeout of the on-screen keyboard, moved lcd_power between
4 and 0 within 200 ms of the DPMS change, the picture came back every
time, and the driver logged nothing.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
